      "text": "## Layer 1: How a Propeller Makes Thrust"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-PROPULSION.md#009",
      "path": [
        "03-PROPULSION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:naval-architecture:03-propulsion, git-history:naval-architecture:03-propulsion]",
        "canonical_path": "naval-architecture/03-PROPULSION.md",
        "concepts": "[propeller, thrust, cavitation, marine engine, propulsive efficiency]",
        "current_path": "naval-architecture/03-PROPULSION.md",
        "id": "maxim:naval-architecture:propulsion",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "naval-architecture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[marine propulsion]",
        "section": "naval-architecture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Marine Propulsion",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "A propeller is a set of rotating wings (blades). Each blade section is an airfoil moving\nthrough water; it generates lift, and the axial component of that lift, summed over all\nblades, is **thrust**. Equivalently — and this is the cleaner mental model — the propeller\naccelerates a column of water aft, and by Newton's third law the reaction pushes the ship\nforward. Both pictures are correct; they are the blade-element view and the momentum view."
    },
